-- title:
--   Geometric fibre of the edge family on the η-branch
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, write $W(k)$ for its Witt vectors, $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ (the type `Zp2 p`), and let $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{p^2} \to W(k)$ be a ring homomorphism. For a commutative ring $B$, `pIdeal p B` is the ideal generated by $p$; let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module (a `FormalODModule p`, i.e. a $2$-dimensional commutative formal group law with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ and a uniformiser endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi\circ a = {}^{\sigma}a\circ\varpi$) over $W(k)/pW(k)$, and write $\bar{\jmath} = \bar\iota$ for the composite of $\iota$ with the reduction $W(k)\to W(k)/pW(k)$.
--
--   Hypotheses on $\Phi$: `hΦ` asserts that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{Lie}_0$ and $\mathrm{Lie}_1$ of its Lie algebra are complementary and both invertible; `hΦ4` asserts that $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the action of $p$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ` asserts $\mathrm{Lie}_0(\Phi) \le \ker(\mathrm{lieVarpi}\,\Phi)$, where $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$ is the linear part of $\varpi$ on the Lie algebra; `hcΦ` asserts that the graded pieces `gradedPiece` of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ in degrees $0$ and $1$ (the eigensubgroups where the Teichmüller action of $c\in\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ acts by homothety by $\bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n}$) are complementary, so that the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi = \Phi.\mathrm{toGradedCartierModuleData}$ is defined; $r_\Phi$ is an additive map $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$, and `hrΦ` requires that for every additive $L : D_\Phi.M \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ which is a canonical $L$-map, $r_\Phi$ maps the whole of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece $D_\Phi.\mathrm{etaPiece}\,L\,\_\,0$.
--
--   Let $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ have matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$ (`hg`).
--
--   Edge family data: let $E =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p (W(k)/pW(k))` be the edge chart ring, with its distinguished elements $\xi$ and $\eta$, let $X$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathrm{CartierModule}\,p\,X.F$. The hypotheses on this family are: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}\to E$ obtained from $\iota$ and $W(k)\to W(k)/p \to E$, i.e. $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$ and the matrix of tangent vectors of the $\gamma_i$ has unit determinant; `hγa`, that $\gamma$ has structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p (W(k)/pW(k))` (the edge constants formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$), i.e. for all $i$ and all $N$ the element $\varpi\gamma_i$ agrees, modulo $V^N$, with $\sum_{m<N} V^m\big(a_{m,i}\,\gamma_{\pi(m,i)}\big)$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   Node data: $f_0 : E \to W(k)/pW(k)$ is a ring homomorphism splitting the structure map ($f_0 \circ \mathrm{algebraMap} = \mathrm{id}$, `hf₀`) with $f_0(\xi) = 0$ (`hf₀ξ`) and $f_0(\eta) = 0$ (`hf₀η`). Further, $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and a series $\rho_0$ over $W(k)/pW(k)$ are given with `hρ₀`: $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi \to X\otimes_{f_0} W(k)/pW(k)$ of height $4m$ (an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$). Let $t_0 = (X\otimes_{f_0} W(k)/pW(k),\, m,\, \bar\rho_0)$ be the corresponding rigidified triple, $\bar\rho_0$ being $\rho_0$ pushed along the reduction of $W(k)/pW(k)$ modulo $p$. The hypotheses `hOD₀`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` state respectively that the series component of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $t_0.\Phi\mathrm{bar}$ to $t_0.X\mathrm{bar}$; that for the identity base change the graded pieces of degrees $0$ and $1$ of $t_0.X\mathrm{bar}$ are complementary; and that the same holds for the corresponding reduction of $\Phi$. With $a \in \mathbb{N}$, the normalisation hypothesis `hN` states that for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota, h_{c\Phi}, r_\Phi, \ldots)(\delta_i) = p^{a+m}\cdot v_i,$$
--   where $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is the composite of $r_\Phi$ with the base-change maps of $N$-modules induced by $\Phi \to t_0.\Phi\mathrm{bar} \to t_0.X\mathrm{bar}$, $\delta_i$ is the $i$-th standard basis vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, $v_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0, 0)$ and $v_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}\,\mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1, 0)$, $\bar\gamma_i$ denoting $\gamma_i$ base-changed along $f_0$, then along the reduction modulo $p$, then along the identity.
--
--   Edge isogeny: $\rho_1$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $(X\otimes_{f_0} W(k)/pW(k))\otimes E$ to $X$ whose induced map on Cartier modules is given explicitly by `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1`:
--   $$\rho_1(\bar\gamma_0) = p\,\gamma_0 - V\big(\tau(\eta^{p^2})\,\gamma_1\big), \qquad \rho_1(\bar\gamma_1) = p\,\gamma_1 - V\big(\tau(\xi^{p^2})\,\gamma_0\big),$$
--   where $\bar\gamma_i$ is here $\gamma_i$ base-changed along $f_0$ and then along $\mathrm{algebraMap}$, $\tau$ is the Teichmüller lift into $W(E)$ and $V = \mathrm{verschiebungInt}$; and `hρ₁h` asserts that $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   Finally, $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, and $y : E \to \Omega$ is a ring homomorphism with $y(\xi) = 0$ (`hyξ`) and $y(\eta) \ne 0$ (`hyη`).
--
--   Conclusion. Let $t = (X,\, m+1,\, \bar\rho_1 \circ \bar\rho_0)$ be the rigidified triple over $E$ whose series is the composite of $\rho_0$ pushed along $E \to E/pE$ (through $\mathrm{algebraMap}$) followed by the reduction modulo $pE$ of the series of $\rho_1$, and let $t\otimes_y \Omega$ be its image under $y$ (the formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module base-changed along $y$, the same integer $m+1$, and the series pushed along the induced map $E/pE \to \Omega/p\Omega$). Then there exist a Drinfeld datum $Q$ over $\Omega$ and a Deligne datum $d$ over $\Omega$, both for $\mathcal{O} = \mathbb{Z}_p$, $K = \mathbb{Q}_p$ and $\pi = p$, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, $t\otimes_y\Omega$ satisfies `IsCartierQuadruple` with respect to $\iota$, $h_{c\Phi}$, $r_\Phi$, the structure homomorphism $y \circ \mathrm{algebraMap} \circ (W(k)\to W(k)/pW(k))$, and $Q$; that predicate requires that the series of $t\otimes_y\Omega$ be an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from the reduction of $\Phi$ to the reduction of its formal module, and that there exist $\Omega$-linear isomorphisms $\tau_0 : Q.T_0 \simeq \mathrm{Lie}_0$ and $\tau_1 : Q.T_1 \simeq \mathrm{Lie}_1$ of the invertible modules of $Q$ with the two graded pieces of the Lie algebra, intertwining $Q.\Pi_0$ and $Q.\Pi_1$ with $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$, and such that for every point $x$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$ the lattices $N_0(x)$, $N_1(x)$ consist exactly of those vectors admitting an $\eta$-section of degree $0$, respectively $1$, over a localisation $\Omega_f$ with $f \notin x$, for suitable complements and a canonical $L$-map, together with further clauses (summarised here) identifying the values of $u_0$ and $u_1$ on such sections with the canonical quotient maps of the graded Cartier module data.
--
--   Second, $Q.\mathrm{IsQuadrupleOf}\ d$: for every point $x$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$, the pair of lattices $(Q.L_0(x), Q.L_1(x))$ is edge-nondegenerate at $x$ for $d$, and the kernels of $u_0(x)$ and $u_1(x)$ are the lines assigned by $d$, base-changed to the local ring, to $Q.L_0(x)$ and $Q.L_1(x)$ respectively.
--
--   Third, on the standard full lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \subset \mathbb{Q}_p^2$,
--   $$d.\mathrm{line}(\mathbb{Z}_p^2) = \Omega\cdot\big(y(\xi)\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} e_0 + 1 \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} e_1\big),$$
--   where $e_0, e_1$ are the standard basis vectors of the lattice.
--
--   Fourth, on the translated lattice $g\cdot\mathbb{Z}_p^2$,
--   $$d.\mathrm{line}(g\cdot\mathbb{Z}_p^2) = \big(\Omega\cdot(1\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} e_0 + y(\eta)\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} e_1)\big)\ \text{transported by}\ \mathrm{actBaseChange}\,\Omega\,g\,(\mathbb{Z}_p^2),$$
--   that is, the image of that $\Omega$-line under the isomorphism $\Omega\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to \Omega\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}(g\cdot\mathbb{Z}_p^2)$ induced by $g$.
--
--   This is the computation of the geometric fibre of the rigidified edge family of special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules at a point of the $\eta$-branch of the edge chart, where $y(\xi) = 0$ and $y(\eta) \ne 0$: the Cartier quadruple attached to the fibre is the Drinfeld quadruple of a Deligne datum whose two lines are given explicitly on the standard lattice and on $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, realising the $p$-adic uniformisation dictionary of Čerednik and Drinfeld. It is one of the three cases (node, $\xi$-branch, $\eta$-branch) combined in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny).
